>>
endobj
816 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 316 0 R
/E (Cutting SNAP Would Devastate Communities Across the U.S.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
817 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 316 0 R
/E (The Senate must put Americans first and fully fund programs that combat hunger in the U.S.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
818 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 316 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
819 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 316 0 R
/E (Some may argue that government efficiency is long overdue. House Republicans estimate that cutting Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program funding would save roughly $300 billion. But funding for hunger relief is essential and should not be on the chopping block.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
820 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 316 0 R
/E (There are more than 400 food shelves in Minnesota working to ensure families have access to food. Many rely on federal funding to maintain operations. That support enables food shelves to provide critical services to communities across the state. These programs are effective. Cutting funding would treat hunger as an inevitable consequence of poverty rather than a preventable injustice.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
821 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 316 0 R
/E (All people deserve proper nutrition. Grant-funded programs like SNAP are often a lifeline for food shelves, food banks and third-party organizations that help families put food on the table. According to the Minnesota Department of Human Services, the proposed federal spending reduction on SNAP would mean the state must cover its annual maintenance, which costs $220 million. Food banks have also experienced cuts across the country, including $500 million in government commodity food.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
822 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 316 0 R
/E (Federal cuts to hunger relief programs would result in devastating and lasting impacts on families and communities. If Congress fails to fully fund these services, the consequences will continue to ripple through Minnesota and across the U.S. Hunger is often the result of economic disruption, housing instability or unexpected expenses. Second Harvest Heartland found that roughly 40 percent of those facing hunger are not eligible for federal food assistance. The demand for affordable housing is high, and a lack of options forces families to spend more on housing, leaving less for other basic needs. Inflation has also led to higher costs and reduced purchasing power for those who rely on food shelves.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
823 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 316 0 R
/E (The consequences for children are especially severe. Childhood hunger affects academic performance, physical development and emotional well-being. Children without consistent access to food often struggle in school, are more likely to suffer from health issues such as anemia and asthma, and face emotional burdens such as stress, shame and isolation.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
824 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 316 0 R
/E (When kids are hungry, they worry more about where their next meal will come from and are less able to focus on their future success, including academics, sports and personal interests. Food insecurity can also make it more difficult for children to form friendships, as not having food when others are eating can be embarrassing. Hunger impacts emotions, causing frustration and other challenges.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
825 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 316 0 R
/E (More than 300 food shelves operate in Minnesota, offering free food to local communities. These food shelves, food banks and partner organizations work together to make sure no child goes hungry. When we invest in feeding children today, we are investing in a stronger and healthier Minnesota for future generations.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
